\section{Important notes}{
Here are some important notes for all functions in the \code{glyenzy} package.
\subsection{Applicability}{

Known-enzyme algorithms and enzyme information in glyenzy are applicable only
to humans. Curated coverage is strongest for N-glycans and O-glycans and also
includes selected glycosphingolipid headgroups and other glycan contexts.
Lipid and protein aglycones are not represented, so glycolipid rules model the
carbohydrate headgroup with ceramide omitted. Results may be inaccurate for
unsupported glycan contexts or other species (e.g., plants, insects).
}

\subsection{Inclusiveness}{

The algorithm takes an intentionally inclusive approach,
assuming that all possible isoenzymes capable of catalyzing
a given reaction may be involved.
Therefore, results should be interpreted with caution.

For example, in humans, detection of the motif "Neu5Ac(a2-3)Gal(b1-" will return
both "ST3GAL3" and "ST3GAL4". In reality, only one of them might be active, depending
on factors such as tissue specificity.
}

\subsection{Concrete glycans by default}{

Most functions only work for glycans containing \strong{concrete} residues
(e.g., \code{"Glc"}, \code{"GalNAc"}), and not for glycans with \strong{generic}
residues (e.g., \code{"Hex"}, \code{"HexNAc"}).
Inputs with generic residues are supported where explicitly
documented, such as \code{trace_biosynthesis()} and \code{path_biosynthesis()}.
}

\subsection{Substituents}{

Sulfate substituents are supported. Other substituents, such as
phosphorylation and methylation, are not supported. Use
\code{\link[glyrepr:remove_substituents]{glyrepr::remove_substituents()}} when unsupported substituents are present.
